<h1>About this site</h1>

<h2>Schools are made, not found</h2>

<p>Literary history is full of schools: the New York School, the Black Mountain
poets, the Confessionals. It is tempting to think of them as natural kinds,
groups that existed and waited to be discovered. This site starts from the
opposite view. A school is crafted by its most ardent students. Someone decides
which poets belong, which ties count, and which differences to ignore. The
school is the result of that work.</p>

<p>That makes a "Pittsburgh poet" a claim, not a fact. So this site does not
just list names. For each poet it records the specific ties to the city, each
with a date and a source, and it says why the entry is here. A reader who
disagrees with an inclusion can see exactly where the line was drawn.</p>

<h2>What the catalog is for</h2>

<ul>
  <li>To gather what is known about poets who lived or worked in Pittsburgh.</li>
  <li>To cite a source for every claim.</li>
  <li>To keep the edge cases visible. A poet who was born here and left as an
      infant teaches more about the category than one who never left.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How it is made</h2>

<p>Each entry is a plain text file. Entries are signed and dated. When the
<a href="{{ path('pghpoets_criteria') }}">inclusion criteria</a> change, the
change is logged on that page.</p>

<p>Corrections and additions are welcome.</p>
